\title{Data and Model Scaling, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning, and KV-Cache
Inference in Million-Parameter Language Models: An Empirical Study}

\author{
\IEEEauthorblockN{Adyaan Ahmed}
\IEEEauthorblockA{
School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Sciences (SEECS) \\{}
National University of Sciences and Technology (NUST) \\{}
Islamabad, Pakistan \\{}
aahmed.bsds23seecs@seecs.edu.pk
}
}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Scaling laws, data-repetition trade-offs, low-rank adaptation (LoRA), and key-value (KV) caching are well studied in large language models, but whether their benefits carry over to models with only a few million parameters is not obvious. I train decoder-only Transformers from scratch on the full TinyStories corpus (904M tokens) with a custom word-level BPE tokenizer and run five controlled experiments on one laptop GPU, with seeded runs and per-configuration learning-rate selection throughout. At a fixed 100M-token training budget, shrinking the pool of \emph{unique} data from 100M to 8M tokens -- i.e. training for up to 12.5 epochs instead of one -- costs almost nothing (test perplexity 5.19 vs.\ 5.20), and even 50 epochs of repetition over a 2M-token pool costs only about 2\%. Across seven model sizes from 75K to 8.8M parameters, test loss falls monotonically and is well described by a power law, $L = 0.71 + 103.7\,N^{-0.37}$. When adapting a 1.78M-parameter model to an instruction-following task, LoRA at rank 16 closes 94\% of full fine-tuning's perplexity gap with 14.7\% of the trainable parameters and forgets less of the base distribution, yet no arm -- full fine-tuning included -- learns to follow explicit word constraints (0.6--1.9\% success against a 92.8\% reference ceiling). LoRA trains \emph{slower} than full fine-tuning at this width; a width sweep shows the crossover lies between embedding dimensions 512 and 1024, where LoRA becomes 1.84x faster. KV caching gives no reliable speedup for short single generations but reaches 1.85x at batch 16 and 490 generated tokens, where a preallocated cache also caps peak memory below the uncached path. The takeaway is unchanged but now better supported: parameter efficiency, data efficiency, training-time efficiency, and inference-time efficiency are separate axes that do not automatically move together once model size drops this low.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Tiny language models, decoder-only Transformers, scaling laws, data-constrained scaling, LoRA, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, KV cache, inference efficiency, empirical study.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Small language models trained on narrow, simplified corpora are a cheap setting for studying language model behavior. TinyStories \cite{eldan2023tinystories} showed that models far smaller than production systems can still produce coherent text when trained on a restricted vocabulary and simple narrative structure. I refer to this regime loosely as TinyLMs: decoder-only Transformers on the order of a hundred thousand to a few million parameters, small enough that controlled experiments can be repeated, with multiple seeds, on a single laptop GPU.

A separate line of work studies efficiency at the other end of the scale: scaling laws that predict how loss falls with model and data size \cite{kaplan2020scaling,hoffmann2022chinchilla}, analyses of how repeated data compares with fresh data \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained}, parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods such as LoRA \cite{hu2022lora,houlsby2019peft}, and KV caching, which avoids recomputing attention keys and values at every generation step \cite{shazeer2019multiquery,pope2022efficiently}. These techniques are motivated by the cost of training and serving models with hundreds of millions to hundreds of billions of parameters. Whether they behave the same way at the opposite end of the scale is a separate question: per-layer computation is small, fixed overheads (kernel launch, memory allocation, framework bookkeeping) make up a larger share of total cost, and the trainable-parameter counts involved are already tiny in absolute terms.

This paper does not propose a new method. It asks a narrower empirical question: applied to TinyLMs under a controlled setup, do scaling, data repetition, LoRA, and KV caching behave the way their large-scale reputation would predict?

\textbf{RQ1 (data).} At a fixed training-token budget, how much does the amount of \emph{unique} data matter -- how costly is repeating a smaller pool?

\textbf{RQ2 (model).} Does test loss improve monotonically from 75K to 8.8M parameters, and does a power law describe it?

\textbf{RQ3 (LoRA, quality).} Matched by final architecture and parameter count, how much of full fine-tuning's gain does LoRA recover, at what trainable fraction, and with what side effects on forgetting and instruction following?

\textbf{RQ4 (LoRA, speed).} Does LoRA train faster than full fine-tuning at TinyLM scale -- and if not here, where is the crossover?

\textbf{RQ5 (KV cache).} Does KV caching give meaningful end-to-end generation speedups for TinyLMs, and how does that vary with batch size and generation length?

I answer these with five controlled experiments on models I train and evaluate myself, sharing a tokenizer, data splits, seeding discipline, and evaluation protocol throughout. The contribution is empirical, not algorithmic: parameter efficiency, data efficiency, training-time efficiency, and inference-time efficiency turn out to be distinct axes that do not necessarily move together at this scale. In particular, LoRA's large cut in trainable parameters does not translate into a training-time speedup below a width crossover, and KV caching's benefit is confined to long, batched generations rather than being a default win.

\section{Background}

My model follows the standard decoder-only, GPT-style design \cite{vaswani2017attention,radford2019gpt2}: token embeddings plus learned positional embeddings, $L$ Transformer blocks with causal multi-head self-attention and a position-wise feed-forward network, pre-normalization, and residual connections:
\begin{align}
h' &= h + \text{Attn}(\text{LN}(h)) \\
h'' &= h' + \text{FFN}(\text{LN}(h'))
\end{align}
with causal, scaled dot-product attention
\begin{equation}
\text{Attn}(Q,K,V) = \text{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}} + M\right)V,
\end{equation}
where $M$ masks out future positions. The output head is tied to the input embedding table. None of this architecture is a contribution; it just establishes what I measure.

\textbf{Data-constrained scaling.} Muennighoff et al.\ \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained} showed, for models hundreds of times larger than mine, that repeating data for up to about four epochs is nearly as valuable as fresh data, with diminishing value beyond. Experiment 1 asks whether that conclusion survives at TinyLM scale and much higher repetition counts.

\textbf{LoRA} \cite{hu2022lora} freezes a pretrained weight matrix $W_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times k}$ and represents the update as a low-rank product $W = W_0 + \frac{\alpha}{r}BA$, with $B \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times r}$, $A \in \mathbb{R}^{r \times k}$, and $r \ll \min(d,k)$. Only $A$ and $B$ train; $W_0$ stays fixed. Two consequences matter here. First, LoRA does not by itself reduce the computation performed through $W_0$ during the forward or backward pass -- the frozen matrix still takes part in the full matrix multiplication, and the low-rank path adds computation on top of it; whether freezing pays off in wall-clock terms is therefore a question of regime, not a property of the method (Experiments 3 and 4). Second, because the update is an additive matrix, it can be folded back into $W_0$ after training, so a LoRA-fine-tuned model can be made architecturally identical to a fully fine-tuned one -- which is what makes the comparison in Experiment 3 exact rather than approximate.

\textbf{KV caching} stores the key and value tensors from previous positions during autoregressive generation so only the newest token's key and value need computing at each step \cite{shazeer2019multiquery,pope2022efficiently}. This removes redundant computation but adds memory and bookkeeping cost, and whether the trade is worth it depends on how large the avoided computation is relative to that overhead -- the question Experiment 5 asks directly.

\section{Related Work}

The Transformer \cite{vaswani2017attention} and its decoder-only, GPT-style variant \cite{radford2019gpt2} underlie most modern language models, including the ones here. Kaplan et al.\ \cite{kaplan2020scaling} showed loss follows power laws in model size, data size, and compute; Hoffmann et al.\ \cite{hoffmann2022chinchilla} argued model size and data should scale together. Both operate many orders of magnitude above my setting; Experiment 2 checks whether a power law still describes a seven-point, two-orders-of-magnitude range at the bottom of the scale, and Experiment 1 probes the data side directly under a fixed token budget, complementing the data-constrained analysis of Muennighoff et al.\ \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained}. The BabyLM challenge \cite{warstadt2023babylm} shares the small-data, small-model setting but targets developmentally plausible pretraining quality rather than efficiency trade-offs. Adapter modules \cite{houlsby2019peft} and LoRA \cite{hu2022lora} were proposed to adapt large pretrained models without updating every parameter, motivated by the storage and deployment cost of many full fine-tuned copies; reported results at large scale show competitive quality alongside large parameter savings and, in some settings, better training throughput \cite{hu2022lora}. Experiments 3 and 4 check both halves of that trade-off separately at TinyLM scale, including a forgetting measurement and a task-specific constraint-following metric, which parameter-efficiency studies rarely report. KV caching and related ideas for serving large models efficiently are well covered elsewhere \cite{shazeer2019multiquery,pope2022efficiently}, but that literature assumes large models, long contexts, and high serving throughput -- all different from the short generations studied here. TinyStories \cite{eldan2023tinystories} showed models under ten million parameters can produce coherent short stories on a simplified corpus; I build on that setting but ask different questions, about data, capacity, adaptation, and inference efficiency rather than generation quality alone. None of this work tests scaling, repetition, LoRA, or KV caching directly at the million-parameter scale with short generations, which is the gap this paper fills empirically.

\section{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Data.} I use TinyStories \cite{eldan2023tinystories} and TinyStoriesInstruct. The full TinyStories train split (2,109,719 stories) tokenizes to 904.5M tokens; I carve a 10,000-story dev split (4.27M tokens) out of the official validation split and use the remaining 21,990 stories (9.17M tokens) as test. TinyStoriesInstruct contributes 294,993 training examples (153.5M tokens), 5,000 dev (2.60M), and 4,999 test examples (2.48M, the official validation split). Each Instruct example is an instruction header (\texttt{Features:}/\texttt{Words:}/\texttt{Summary:} fields, ending in \texttt{Story:}) followed by a story; the training loss is masked so only story tokens count. Every story is Unicode-normalized (NFKC), stripped of non-printable characters, whitespace-collapsed, and trimmed; nothing is truncated, filtered, or deduplicated. One known data defect: about 5.6\% of validation stories (1,229 of 21,990) contain a mangled quote sequence (\texttt{\^{a}\texteuro}) from a mis-decoding upstream in TinyStories itself; I left it in place rather than re-run the pipeline, and it visibly surfaces in some generations (Section~\ref{sec:samples}).

\textbf{Tokenizer.} I trained a word-level byte-pair-encoding tokenizer \cite{sennrich2016bpe} from scratch in pure Python on 200,000 training stories: 1,022 learned tokens plus \texttt{<unk>} and \texttt{<|endoftext|>}, a 1,024-token vocabulary. Words are split on whitespace before merging, so tokens never cross word boundaries, and token IDs are assigned deterministically so every process sees the same vocabulary. This vocabulary is small next to production tokenizers (tens of thousands of tokens); I return to what that might mean in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}. The same tokenizer is used everywhere in the paper, so it is never a confound between conditions.

\textbf{Model family.} All models share the architecture of Section II. For the model-scaling experiment I vary depth $L$ with $\text{heads} = L$ and $\text{embed\_dim} = 16L$ (head dimension fixed at 16), spanning 74,624 to 8,814,848 parameters. Experiments 1 and 3 use the 8-layer member of this family (8 heads, 128-dimensional embeddings, 1,783,040 parameters).

\textbf{Training.} AdamW \cite{loshchilov2019decoupled} with $\beta = (0.9, 0.95)$, weight decay 0.01 applied to 2D-and-larger parameters only, gradient clipping at 1.0, dropout 0.1, context length 512, batch size 32. The learning rate follows linear warmup (5--10\% of steps) then cosine decay to 10\% of peak. Every run is fully seeded: initialization and the batch sequence both derive from the run's seed, and a seeded epoch-based sampler guarantees every window in the training pool is seen exactly once per epoch, so two runs given the same seed see byte-identical batches. Learning rates are not assumed transferable between configurations: each model size (Experiment 2) and each fine-tuning arm (Experiment 3) first runs a short dev-set probe per candidate learning rate from a fixed grid, all candidates starting from identical initialization and seeing identical batches, and trains at the winner.

\textbf{Evaluation.} Test loss and perplexity are computed exactly (every non-overlapping window, no sampling) over a fixed prefix of each test split: the first 2M of the 9.17M TinyStories test tokens for Experiments 1 and 2, and the first 1M tokens of each test set for Experiment 3. These are deterministic given a checkpoint, but they are 22--40\% of the full splits, not the whole thing -- Section~\ref{sec:limitations} is explicit about this. Multi-seed results are reported as mean $\pm$ 95\% confidence interval across seeds (3 seeds in Experiments 1--2, 5 in Experiment 3).

\textbf{Hardware and timing.} Everything ran on one machine: an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 6GB Laptop GPU, PyTorch 2.5.1 (CUDA), Python 3.11.13. Timed sections exclude warmup, call \texttt{torch.cuda.synchronize()} before clock reads, and interleave conditions in a shuffled order so thermal or scheduling drift cannot correlate with the condition being measured; medians over repeats are reported. Experiment 3's fine-tuned checkpoints were saved and re-evaluated in a separate process, reproducing the stored perplexities to three decimals before any sample text was generated from them.

\textbf{Reproducibility.} Every run writes a JSON with its full configuration, seed, git commit, learning curve, and final metrics; every figure and table in this paper is generated from those files by script (\texttt{analysis.py}, \texttt{generate\_figures.py}), never hand-typed.

\section{Experiment 1: Data Scaling at a Fixed Token Budget}
\label{sec:exp1}

How much unique data does a TinyLM actually need? I fix the training budget at 100M tokens (6,103 steps) of the 1.78M-parameter model and vary only the size of the unique pool the budget is drawn from: 2M, 8M, 32M, or 100M tokens. Smaller pools are simply repeated -- the 2M pool is seen 50 times, the 8M pool 12.5 times -- with the sampler re-shuffling each epoch. Pools are prefixes of the train file, and the learning rate is held at $3\times10^{-4}$ for all four conditions so that data amount is the only deliberate variable (a choice with consequences; see Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Data scaling: test perplexity at a fixed 100M-token training budget, varying how many of those tokens are unique (mean $\pm$ 95\% CI, 3 seeds).}
\label{tab:data}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Unique tokens & Epochs & Test PPL & $\Delta$ vs.\ 100M \\
\midrule
2M   & 50.0  & $5.29 \pm 0.05$ & $+1.9\%$ \\
8M   & 12.5  & $5.20 \pm 0.03$ & $+0.1\%$ \\
32M  & 3.1   & $5.18 \pm 0.08$ & $-0.2\%$ \\
100M & 1.0   & $5.19 \pm 0.03$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e1_data_scaling.png}
\caption{Test perplexity vs.\ unique-pool size at a fixed 100M-token budget. Points: individual seeds. The curve flattens after 8M unique tokens (12.5 epochs of repetition).}
\label{fig:data}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:data} and Fig.~\ref{fig:data} show the result: nearly all of the benefit of fresh data arrives by 8M unique tokens. Going from 2M to 8M unique (50 $\to$ 12.5 epochs) improves perplexity by 1.7\%; from 8M to 100M unique (12.5 $\to$ 1 epoch) changes it by less than the seed noise. Even the extreme case -- reading the same 2M tokens fifty times -- lands within about 2\% of the full-data run. This is consistent in direction with data-constrained scaling at large model sizes \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained}, where a few epochs of repetition are nearly free, but the tolerance here is much higher: at this scale and for this many total steps, the model is far from memorizing even heavily repeated data, and unique data past roughly eight epochs' worth buys nothing measurable. For RQ1: at fixed compute, unique data matters up to a point and then stops; repetition is a surprisingly cheap substitute.

\section{Experiment 2: Model Scaling Across Two Orders of Magnitude}
\label{sec:exp2}

I train seven sizes of the model family (depth $L$, heads $L$, width $16L$) from scratch, each on a single pass over 200M tokens (12,207 steps, 22\% of the corpus), with the learning rate swept per size over $\{3\times10^{-4}, 10^{-3}, 3\times10^{-3}\}$ on short dev probes and three seeds per size.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Model scaling: test loss after one 200M-token pass (mean $\pm$ 95\% CI, 3 seeds). Perplexity computed from mean loss. Best LR per size selected on dev probes.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
$L$ & Width & Params & Best LR & Test loss & PPL \\
\midrule
2  & 32  & 74,624    & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $2.322 \pm 0.025$ & 10.20 \\
4  & 64  & 298,368   & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.702 \pm 0.086$ & 5.48 \\
6  & 96  & 818,688   & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.348 \pm 0.002$ & 3.85 \\
8  & 128 & 1,783,040 & $1{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.225 \pm 0.003$ & 3.40 \\
10 & 160 & 3,338,880 & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.089 \pm 0.004$ & 2.97 \\
12 & 192 & 5,633,664 & $1{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.045 \pm 0.004$ & 2.84 \\
14 & 224 & 8,814,848 & $1{\times}10^{-3}$ & $0.995 \pm 0.005$ & 2.70 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e2_scaling_law.png}
\caption{Test loss vs.\ parameter count (log scale), with the fitted power law $L(N) = 0.71 + 103.7\,N^{-0.37}$. Error bars: 95\% CI over 3 seeds; individual seeds plotted.}
\label{fig:scaling}
\end{figure}

Loss falls monotonically across the whole 118x range (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}, Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}), with no plateau and no reversal -- including between the 1.7M model this paper's other experiments are built on and its neighbors. A power law of the standard form $L(N) = E + A\,N^{-\alpha}$ \cite{kaplan2020scaling} fits the seed-averaged losses well: $E = 0.71$, $A = 103.7$, $\alpha = 0.37$. The bootstrap confidence intervals are wide (95\% CI for $\alpha$: 0.22--0.57; for $E$: 0.30--0.91), as expected for a seven-point fit, so I treat the curve as a good description of the trend rather than a precise exponent estimate. For RQ2: even at the bottom of the parameter range, and with a 1,024-token vocabulary, capacity scaling behaves the way large-scale experience says it should -- smoothly, predictably, and without any visible floor in the range tested.

\section{Experiment 3: LoRA vs.\ Full Fine-Tuning, Matched Exactly}
\label{sec:exp3}

I take the 8-layer, 1.78M-parameter model from Experiment 2 (seed 0; TinyStories test perplexity 3.40, Instruct test perplexity 3.65 before adaptation) and adapt it to TinyStoriesInstruct -- a task with a measurable behavioral component, not just a distribution shift. Every arm trains for 1,220 steps (20M tokens, 13\% of the Instruct train split) with the loss masked to story tokens, from an identical deep copy of the base checkpoint, at a learning rate swept per arm (LoRA arms over $\{3{\times}10^{-4}, \ldots, 10^{-2}\}$, full fine-tuning over $\{3{\times}10^{-5}, \ldots, 10^{-3}\}$), with 5 seeds. LoRA wraps all four linear layers per block ($\alpha = 2r$), and after training the adapter is merged back into the base weights, so the evaluated LoRA model has \emph{exactly} the architecture and parameter count of the fully fine-tuned one. Three outcomes are measured: Instruct test perplexity, TinyStories test perplexity (a forgetting check), and a Words-constraint rate -- the fraction of 500 held-out Instruct headers whose generated story contains all three target words, where the reference stories written for those headers set a 92.8\% ceiling.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{LoRA vs.\ full fine-tuning, adapting the 1.78M base model to TinyStoriesInstruct (means over 5 seeds; 95\% CIs are $\leq$0.002 for perplexity and $\leq$0.7pp for Words rate). Gap closed: share of the baseline$\to$full-FT perplexity gap recovered. Words ceiling (reference stories): 92.8\%.}
\label{tab:lora}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
Arm & Train.\ par.\ (\%) & PPL & Forget & Words & Time \\
\midrule
Baseline   & 0 (0\%)        & 3.650 & 3.398 & 0.0\%  & 0s \\
LoRA $r{=}1$  & 16K (0.9\%)   & 3.287 & 3.444 & 0.6\%  & 306s \\
LoRA $r{=}2$  & 33K (1.8\%)   & 3.268 & 3.447 & 0.6\%  & 313s \\
LoRA $r{=}4$  & 66K (3.7\%)   & 3.256 & 3.449 & 0.6\%  & 313s \\
LoRA $r{=}8$  & 131K (7.3\%)  & 3.250 & 3.453 & 1.3\%  & 312s \\
LoRA $r{=}16$ & 262K (14.7\%) & 3.230 & 3.444 & 1.9\%  & 308s \\
LoRA $r{=}32$ & 524K (29.4\%) & 3.221 & 3.447 & 1.7\%  & 317s \\
LoRA $r{=}64$ & 1.05M (58.8\%)& 3.211 & 3.442 & 1.8\%  & 323s \\
Full FT    & 1.78M (100\%)  & \textbf{3.201} & 3.474 & 1.4\%  & \textbf{254s} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e3_lora_vs_fullft.png}
\caption{Instruct test perplexity vs.\ LoRA trainable-parameter count (log scale), against the no-fine-tuning and full-fine-tuning references.}
\label{fig:lora}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e3_forgetting.png}
\caption{Forgetting check: TinyStories test perplexity after adaptation (baseline: 3.398). Full fine-tuning forgets the most; all LoRA ranks cluster slightly lower.}
\label{fig:forget}
\end{figure}

Four findings (Table~\ref{tab:lora}, Figs.~\ref{fig:lora} and~\ref{fig:forget}). First, LoRA works \emph{very} cheaply at this scale: rank 1 -- 0.9\% of the parameters -- already closes 81\% of the perplexity gap between not fine-tuning and full fine-tuning, and rank 16 closes 94\% with 14.7\%. Second, full fine-tuning still wins on raw perplexity (3.201) and pays for it with the most forgetting of the base distribution (3.474 vs.\ 3.44--3.45 for every LoRA rank). Third, and most striking, the behavioral metric barely moves: every arm, full fine-tuning included, satisfies all three word constraints on only 0.6--1.9\% of prompts, against a 92.8\% reference ceiling. Twenty million tokens of instruction tuning taught the models the \emph{genre} (Section~\ref{sec:samples} shows they reliably open with ``Once upon a time\ldots'' and pick up names from the summary) but not explicit constraint following; per-arm differences at this rate are within noise (500 prompts, one sample each). Fourth -- the result that motivates Experiment 4 -- LoRA is \emph{slower} in wall-clock terms than full fine-tuning at every rank (306--323s vs.\ 254s), despite training up to 109x fewer parameters. One consistent pattern in the LR sweeps is worth reporting: the preferred LoRA learning rate falls with rank, from $10^{-2}$ at ranks 1--8 to $10^{-3}$ at rank 64, approaching full fine-tuning's $3{\times}10^{-4}$ -- the larger the rank, the more the update behaves like ordinary training.

For RQ3: LoRA delivers extreme parameter efficiency and slightly less forgetting, full fine-tuning still takes the best perplexity, and neither produces measurable constraint-following at this scale. Fewer trainable parameters also did not mean faster training -- which deserves its own experiment.

\section{Experiment 4: Where Does LoRA Actually Train Faster?}
\label{sec:exp4}

Experiment 3's timing result has a simple candidate explanation: at width 128, the frozen base matrices are cheap, so the gradient compute LoRA saves is small while its low-rank path adds fixed overhead on every layer. If so, the ranking should flip as width grows and matmuls dominate. I benchmark training-step time directly: 8-layer models at widths 64--1024 (0.5M--102M parameters, head dimension fixed at 64), full fine-tuning vs.\ LoRA rank 8 and rank 64, on random token batches (random weights; this measures compute, not learning). Each condition runs 50 warmup plus 200 timed steps at batch 16, repeated 5 times in shuffled interleaved order; medians over all timed steps are reported.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Median training-step time by model width (8 layers, batch 16, context 512). Speedup is full-FT time divided by LoRA time.}
\label{tab:width}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
Width & Params & Full & LoRA $r{=}8$ & LoRA $r{=}64$ & $r{=}8$ speedup \\
\midrule
64   & 0.50M  & 0.108s & 0.121s & 0.127s & 0.89x \\
128  & 1.78M  & 0.218s & 0.238s & 0.248s & 0.92x \\
256  & 6.71M  & 0.765s & 0.742s & 0.765s & 1.03x \\
512  & 26.0M  & 1.046s & 1.157s & 1.277s & 0.90x \\
1024 & 102.3M & 5.040s & \textbf{2.737s} & 3.061s & \textbf{1.84x} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e4_train_speed.png}
\caption{Median training-step time vs.\ model width. LoRA is slower up to width 512 and clearly faster at width 1024; the spread of points at width 1024 shows repeat-to-repeat drift on the laptop GPU.}
\label{fig:width}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:width} and Fig.~\ref{fig:width} confirm the mechanism: LoRA is 9--12\% \emph{slower} per step at widths 64--128 (Experiment 3's regime), roughly at parity at width 256, and 1.84x (rank 8) / 1.65x (rank 64) \emph{faster} at width 1024, where the model has 102M parameters and the frozen-weight gradient savings finally outweigh the adapter overhead. Repeat-to-repeat drift is visible at the largest width (points spread between roughly 3.7s and 6.1s across repeats of full fine-tuning), consistent with thermal behavior of a laptop GPU under sustained load; the ordering of methods is stable across repeats nonetheless. For RQ4: ``LoRA trains faster'' is not a property of LoRA but of the regime -- below a width crossover it is pure overhead, above it the savings are real. At 1.78M parameters, Experiment 3's arms sit on the wrong side of that line.

\section{Experiment 5: KV-Cache Inference}
\label{sec:exp5}

I measure end-to-end generation latency for the 1.78M-parameter model shape across three generation paths implemented in the codebase: no cache (full re-forward per step), a concatenating KV cache, and a preallocated write-in-place KV cache. Generation is greedy from a random 16-token prompt (random weights; again this measures compute, not text quality), for 20--490 new tokens at batch size 1 or 16, with 10 repeats per condition, interleaved in shuffled order; medians and peak memory are recorded.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{KV-cache results at batch 16 (median of 10 repeats). Speedup: no-cache time / cached time. Peak memory for the no-cache path grows with generation length; the preallocated cache holds it constant.}
\label{tab:kvcache}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrr}
\toprule
Gen.\ tokens & No cache & Concat & Prealloc & Prealloc speedup \\
\midrule
20  & 0.140s & 0.139s (1.01x) & 0.162s & 0.86x \\
50  & 0.342s & 0.257s (1.33x) & 0.320s & 1.07x \\
100 & 0.588s & 0.622s (0.94x) & 0.722s & 0.81x \\
250 & 1.911s & 1.795s (1.06x) & 1.437s & 1.33x \\
490 & 6.348s & 3.428s (1.85x) & \textbf{3.390s} & \textbf{1.87x} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e5_kvcache_speedup.png}
\caption{KV-cache speedup vs.\ generation length, two cache implementations at batch 1 (dotted) and batch 16 (solid). Values below the dashed line mean caching was slower. Short generations hover at 1.0x; the benefit appears at batch 16 with long generations.}
\label{fig:kvcache}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kvcache} and Fig.~\ref{fig:kvcache} show a sharp regime split. Up to 100 generated tokens, caching does nothing reliably: speedups scatter around 1.0x (0.81--1.33x) and inter-quartile ranges straddle the break-even line, at both batch sizes. At batch 1 the picture stays noisy even at 490 tokens (1.20x median, with quartiles overlapping 1.0x). The clear win appears only at batch 16 with 490 generated tokens: 1.85x (concat) and 1.87x (prealloc), tightly separated from the no-cache distribution. Memory tells the same story from the other side: at batch 16 the no-cache path's peak memory grows from 30MB to 108MB as generation lengthens (each step re-runs the full prefix), while the preallocated cache holds peak memory constant at 93.4MB -- the KV tensors themselves cost only 66MB even in the largest configuration. For RQ5: at TinyLM scale KV caching is neither useless nor a default win; it is a tool for long, batched generation, exactly where the avoided attention compute finally dwarfs the per-step bookkeeping.

\section{What the Models Actually Write}
\label{sec:samples}

Numbers miss what the failure modes look like, so I regenerated Experiment 3's seed-0 fine-tuned models (each reproduces its stored Instruct perplexity to three decimals) and sampled from every arm on the same held-out prompts (temperature 0.8, top-$k$ 50). Table~\ref{tab:samples} shows one representative prompt; the full set is in the repository.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Sample continuations for the prompt ``\texttt{Words: borrow, crystal, strong Summary: Lily accidentally breaks her friend's crystal necklace\ldots\ Story:}'' (target words \textbf{borrow, crystal, strong}). Unfiltered, one sample per arm.}
\label{tab:samples}
\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{13.2cm}}
\toprule
Arm & Generated continuation (trimmed) \\
\midrule
No fine-tuning & ``That's important to be careful when borrows, okay?'' -- a fragment, not a story; the base model does not recognize the header format. \emph{Words used: borrow.} \\
LoRA $r{=}64$ & ``Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily. She loved to play outside. One day, she went to the park\ldots\ She saw a big train\ldots\ She tried the borrow but she couldn't catch it. But it was too high. She tried to grab the borrow but it was too strong\ldots\ and she went to the park to get her crystal.'' -- all three words used, but as near-nonsense (``the borrow'' as a noun). \emph{Words used: borrow, crystal, strong.} \\
Full fine-tuning & ``Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily\ldots\ she went to the park to play. She saw a big train in the park. She wanted to try it. She tried the bath too, but it was too fast. Lily tried to pull the bath too hard for her. Suddenly, the bath got stuck and it hurt her\ldots'' -- fluent, on-genre, and ignores all three target words entirely. \emph{Words used: none.} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Three qualitative observations match the metrics. First, adaptation is visibly real: every fine-tuned arm opens with ``Once upon a time\ldots'' and picks up names and objects from the prompt's summary field, which the base model never does. Second, constraint following is exactly as weak as the Words rate says -- when target words appear at all, they are often grammatically forced (``the borrow''). Third, the characteristic failure modes are repetition loops (``a picture of a picture of a picture''), stories that run past their ending into a fresh \texttt{Summary:} header, and the occasional mangled \texttt{\^{a}\texteuro} sequence inherited from the source data (Section IV).

\section{Discussion}

Five experiments point at one idea: efficiency is not one thing. Parameter count, unique data, training time, inference time, memory, and model quality moved independently across these experiments, and each large-scale assumption I tested needed a regime qualifier at this scale. Data repetition, supposedly costly, was nearly free up to twelve epochs. Capacity scaling, the assumption that \emph{did} transfer cleanly, followed a smooth power law all the way down to 75K parameters. LoRA delivered its parameter savings and a small forgetting benefit, but not the training-speed benefit people casually assume -- until the model got wide enough, where the same code showed a 1.84x speedup. KV caching, built to cut inference cost, was dead weight for short single generations and a clear 1.87x win for long batched ones, with a memory-cap effect that matters more than speed on a 6GB card.

The LoRA width crossover and the KV-cache length crossover are the results most worth remembering, because both are cases where the folklore answer (``fewer trainable parameters means faster training''; ``caching is how you make generation fast'') is silently conditional on scale. Neither condition is exotic or hidden; both are just usually satisfied at the scales where these techniques are discussed. At TinyLM scale they are not, and the only reliable way to know is to measure.

None of this is a claim that LoRA is slower in general, or that KV caching does not help small models -- only that, in this specific controlled setup, benefits usually assumed by default did not appear until the regime changed, and the regime change was measurable.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Several limitations qualify the numbers above, and I list them rather than smooth them over. (1) Test metrics use fixed prefixes, not full splits: the first 2M of 9.17M TinyStories test tokens (Experiments 1--2) and the first 1M tokens of each test set (Experiment 3) -- 22--40\% of each split, deterministic but partial. (2) Experiment 1 logged against the test split during training rather than the dev split; nothing selected on those values (fixed learning rate, no early stopping), so the results stand, but the routing was wrong. (3) Experiment 1's pools are prefixes of the train file in the dataset's original story order, not random subsets, and its learning rate was fixed at $3{\times}10^{-4}$ rather than swept per pool size -- the flat 8M-to-100M region could in principle hide a learning-rate interaction. (4) Every number comes from one laptop-class GPU; Experiments 4 and 5 show visible repeat-to-repeat thermal drift, use random weights and random token batches (legitimate for timing, silent about learning), and fix depth at 8 layers while sweeping width. (5) The Words-constraint rate uses 500 prompts with one sample each; at success rates around 1\%, per-arm differences are noise and only the floor (0\%) and ceiling (92.8\%) are firm. (6) The Experiment 2 power law is a seven-point fit over a 118x range with wide bootstrap intervals; it describes, it does not extrapolate. (7) The 1,024-token vocabulary, the single text domain, the 512-token context, and the un-cleaned mojibake in the source data all bound how far these conclusions generalize. (8) I measured peak memory only in Experiment 5; training-time memory for LoRA vs.\ full fine-tuning remains unmeasured.

\section{Conclusion}

At a fixed 100M-token budget, unique data stopped mattering past about 8M tokens; twelve epochs of repetition cost nothing measurable and fifty epochs cost 2\%. Test loss fell smoothly from 75K to 8.8M parameters along a power law with no visible floor. LoRA recovered up to 98\% of full fine-tuning's perplexity gain with a fraction of the trainable parameters and forgot less, but trained slower at this width -- the speed advantage only appears past a width crossover near 26M parameters -- and no amount of adaptation produced real constraint-following, only genre. KV caching earned its keep only for long, batched generations, where it also capped memory. Each of these is a small, bounded, measured claim; together they make one general one: efficiency techniques carry regime conditions that are usually invisible at the scale where they are proposed, and re-measuring them at the scale where they will actually run is cheap enough that there is no excuse not to. Obvious next steps are full-split evaluation, more model families and seeds, cross-hardware benchmarks, real prompt distributions for the inference measurements, and a data-cleaning pass over TinyStories.

